A||||5||MrPBody||Clutch or hydraulics? (ramble)||||5||
Z||000000||MrPBody||10-23-2001||08:44 PM||||<B>I have clutch gremlins!</B> Without thrashing through all the details, as infamously I do . . .<P>My clutch ('85 SE 2.5) abruptly stopped releasing fully.  I replaced master and slave cylinders.  (They needed replacing anyhow.)  I'm pretty sure I purged all air from the system -- with the slave cylinder piston resting agiainst the clip at the end of its bore, the pedal feels like a brick -- but the clutch still does not release fully, and the effort feels lighter than before.<P>BTW, I don't fully accept the mantra "You gotta buy genuine GM clutch hydraulics."  As far as I can tell, "Genuine GM" = Girling, and -- as a former British car owner and mechanic -- I'm familiar with the failings of Gurgling hydraulics (and its Japanese subsidiary Ushi-Gushi.)  But then, maybe that's why my clutch isn't working.<P>But I digress . . .<P>My question is: Has anyone experienced this kind of clutch failure with a 4-cylinder / Isuzu 5-speed?  That is, have you seen a mechanical failure of the clutch that feels like a hydraulic problem?  I understand that hypothetically it could happen, but does anyone have first-hand evidence?  . . . <I>Can I get a witness?</I><P>Thanks in advance.<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||MrPBody||10-24-2001||09:22 AM||||Bump<P>Isn't someone going to tell me that I'm full of crap, and I <I>really</I> need to buy GM parts?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Bob Englert||10-24-2001||09:43 AM||||Check the travel of the slave cyl pushrod to see if your hydraulics are OK.  If you are getting less than about 15/16" of travel of the rod, then you still have problems there.  More than 1" of travel is the goal (I think it is supposed to be about 1.2" with all new parts and no air in the system).  <P>If you have more than 1" or travel, then I would suspect something mechanically wrong with the clutch.<P>Good luck, this is no fun to fix IMO.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000003||MrPBody||10-24-2001||09:42 PM||||I'll check that.  Thanks!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||MrPBody||10-25-2001||10:31 PM||||<I>Hmmmmmm</I> . . .  Pushrod travels about 1/2".<P>It sure would be a pisser to change the clutch when it doesn't need to be done.<P>looks like I'll pursue hydraulics problems.  I may have to eat my words about GM parts.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||RussB||10-26-2001||12:14 AM||||I have had the same problem, except I tried bleeding mine for three months now. I finally broke down and to the Chevy dealer and ordered GM parts. I'll find out Tuesday if its a problem with the aftermarket parts. If it still doesn't work I'll be looking for a stick of dynamite.||||reg||1
